#{[17592186064559 :question/worked-solution "Apply the sum and constant multiple rules, using the power rule or the standard trigonometric/exponential antiderivative for each term:\n\n$$\n\\int - \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}theta=\\cos θ + C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the result to check:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}theta}\\left(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}+C\\right)=- \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.\n$$"] [17592186064560 :question/worked-solution "Apply the sum and constant multiple rules, using the power rule or the standard trigonometric/exponential antiderivative for each term:\n\n$$\n\\int 3 \\sec^{2}{\\left(z \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}z=3\\tan z + C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the result to check:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}z}\\left(\\frac{3 \\sin{\\left(z \\right)}}{\\cos{\\left(z \\right)}}+C\\right)=\\frac{3 \\sin^{2}{\\left(z \\right)}}{\\cos^{2}{\\left(z \\right)}} + 3.\n$$"] [17592186064561 :question/worked-solution "Apply the sum and constant multiple rules, using the power rule or the standard trigonometric/exponential antiderivative for each term:\n\n$$\n\\int 2 \\csc^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}theta=-2\\cot θ + C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the result to check:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}theta}\\left(- \\frac{2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}+C\\right)=2 + \\frac{2 \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}.\n$$"] [17592186064562 :question/worked-solution "Apply the sum and constant multiple rules, using the power rule or the standard trigonometric/exponential antiderivative for each term:\n\n$$\n\\int - 2 \\cot{\\left(y \\right)} \\csc{\\left(y \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}y=2\\csc y + C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the result to check:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}y}\\left(\\frac{2}{\\sin{\\left(y \\right)}}+C\\right)=- \\frac{2 \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}}{\\sin^{2}{\\left(y \\right)}}.\n$$"] [17592186064563 :question/worked-solution "Apply the sum and constant multiple rules, using the power rule or the standard trigonometric/exponential antiderivative for each term:\n\n$$\n\\int - 3 \\tan{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\sec{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}theta=-3\\sec θ + C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the result to check:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}theta}\\left(- \\frac{3}{\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}+C\\right)=- \\frac{3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}.\n$$"] [17592186064627 :question/worked-solution "Move every term to the left, combine like terms, and factor:\n\n$$\nx^{2} - 5 x + 4=1 \\left(x - 1\\right) \\left(x - 4\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nThe zero-product rule gives\n\n$$\nx = 1, 4.\n$$\n\nSubstitution in the original equation confirms each listed root."] [17592186064628 :question/worked-solution "Move every term to the left, combine like terms, and factor:\n\n$$\nz^{2} + 5 z - 14=1 \\left(z + 7\\right) \\left(z - 2\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nThe zero-product rule gives\n\n$$\nz =-7, 2.\n$$\n\nSubstitution in the original equation confirms each listed root."] [17592186064632 :question/worked-solution "Move every term to the left, combine like terms, and factor:\n\n$$\n- z^{2} + 8 z - 15=- \\left(z - 5\\right) \\left(z - 3\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nThe zero-product rule gives\n\n$$\nz = 3, 5.\n$$\n\nSubstitution in the original equation confirms each listed root."] [17592186064633 :question/worked-solution "Move every term to the left, combine like terms, and factor:\n\n$$\n- x^{2} - 4 x + 5=- \\left(x - 1\\right) \\left(x + 5\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nThe zero-product rule gives\n\n$$\nx =-5, 1.\n$$\n\nSubstitution in the original equation confirms each listed root."] [17592186064713 :question/worked-solution "Apply the sum and constant multiple rules, using the power rule or the standard trigonometric/exponential antiderivative for each term:\n\n$$\n\\int \\sqrt[3]{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{3x^{4/3}}{4} + C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the result to check:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\frac{3 x^{\\frac{4}{3}}}{4}+C\\right)=\\sqrt[3]{x}.\n$$"] [17592186064722 :question/worked-solution "Apply the sum and constant multiple rules, using the power rule or the standard trigonometric/exponential antiderivative for each term:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x^{\\frac{2}{3}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=3x^{1/3} + C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the result to check:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(3 \\sqrt[3]{x}+C\\right)=\\frac{1}{x^{\\frac{2}{3}}}.\n$$"] [17592186064723 :question/worked-solution "Apply the sum and constant multiple rules, using the power rule or the standard trigonometric/exponential antiderivative for each term:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x^{\\frac{3}{5}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{5}{2}x^{2/5} + C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the result to check:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\frac{5 x^{\\frac{2}{5}}}{2}+C\\right)=\\frac{1}{x^{\\frac{3}{5}}}.\n$$"] [17592186064783 :question/worked-solution "Move every term to the left, combine like terms, and factor:\n\n$$\ny^{2} - 11 y + 30=1 \\left(y - 5\\right) \\left(y - 6\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nThe zero-product rule gives\n\n$$\ny = 5, 6.\n$$\n\nSubstitution in the original equation confirms each listed root."] [17592186064848 :question/worked-solution "Apply the sum and constant multiple rules, using the power rule or the standard trigonometric/exponential antiderivative for each term:\n\n$$\n\\int \\left(- 2 \\cot{\\left(x \\right)} + \\csc{\\left(x \\right)}\\right) \\csc{\\left(x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=-\\cot x + 2\\csc x + C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the result to check:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(- \\cot{\\left(x \\right)} + 2 \\csc{\\left(x \\right)}+C\\right)=\\cot^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 \\cot{\\left(x \\right)} \\csc{\\left(x \\right)} + 1.\n$$"] [17592186064849 :question/worked-solution "Apply the sum and constant multiple rules, using the power rule or the standard trigonometric/exponential antiderivative for each term:\n\n$$\n\\int \\left(\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} + 8 \\sec{\\left(x \\right)}\\right) \\sec{\\left(x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\sec x + 8\\tan x + C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the result to check:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(8 \\tan{\\left(x \\right)} + \\sec{\\left(x \\right)}+C\\right)=8 \\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} + \\tan{\\left(x \\right)} \\sec{\\left(x \\right)} + 8.\n$$"] [17592186064961 :question/worked-solution "Move every term to the left, combine like terms, and factor:\n\n$$\nq^{2} + 10 q + 21=1 \\left(q + 3\\right) \\left(q + 7\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nThe zero-product rule gives\n\n$$\nq =-7,-3.\n$$\n\nSubstitution in the original equation confirms each listed root."] [17592186065372 :tutorial/content "To compute the integral of any power function $x^n,$ where $n \\neq -1,$ we can use the **power rule for integration**. The power rule for integration states that\n\n$$\n\\int x^n\\, \\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac {x^{n + 1}}{n + 1} + C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is any constant and $n\\neq -1.$ For example, in the case of $n=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{3+1}}{3+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the **antiderivative** of $f'(x) = x^3$ is $f(x) = \\dfrac {x^{4}}{4}+C .$ \n\nWe can check that this is the correct answer by taking the derivative of $f(x)$ and seeing that it is indeed equal to $x^3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{x}^{4})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4{x}^{4-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{3}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the constant $C$ disappeared when we differentiated because the derivative of any constant is zero. We always add $+C$ whenever we find the antiderivative of a function because we know that it will disappear when we differentiate.\n\n**Note:** The power rule for integration is just the power rule for differentiation in reverse!\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} \\left( x^{n+1} \\right) = (n+1)x^n \\quad \\Leftrightarrow \\quad \\displaystyle \\int x^n \\, \\textrm dx = \\dfrac{x^{n+1}}{n+1}\n$$"] [17592186065649 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186065652] [17592186066159 :answer-field/choices #edb/ref 17592186725821] [17592186066159 :answer-field/type #edb/ref 17592186045492] [17592186066159 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186725821] [17592186066159 :answer-field/id #uuid "60ee00e3-3bb1-5d2c-aa0b-07347ca77762"] [17592186066160 :answer-field/choices #edb/ref 17592186725819] [17592186066160 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186725819] [17592186066160 :answer-field/id #uuid "bffaf690-88e7-5eb5-b99f-92e331f7d793"]}
